          %title
          %p Here are abstracts of talks given at
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Oberwolfach+Workshop%2C+June+2009+--+Strings%2C+Fields%2C+Topology"} Oberwolfach Workshop, June 2009 – Strings, Fields, Topology
          %h2#thomas_schick_smooth_cohomology_theories
            Thomas Schick:
            %em Smooth cohomology theories
          %ul
            %li
              See also
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Oberwolfach+Workshop%2C+June+2009+--+Tuesday%2C+June+9"} notes from the conference
          %p
            The basic idea of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
            (also called
            %em smooth cohomology
            ) is to combine generalized
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
            (e.g. ordinary integral cohomology, or K-theory, or tmf, or …) and differential forms. Hopkins and Singer showed that a smooth refinement exists for any generalized cohomology theory, but didn’t provide an (easy for mere mortals to understand) explicit construction. In this talk, Thomas Schick advertised geometric models for multiplicative smooth cohomology with
            = succeed "-integration" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
            = succeed "-theory" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
          %h2#ullrich_bunke_smooth_ktheory
            Ullrich Bunke:
            %em Smooth K-theory
          %ul
            %li
              See also
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Oberwolfach+Workshop%2C+June+2009+--+Tuesday%2C+June+9"} notes from the conference
          %p
            In this talk, which led on from the previous one by Thomas Schick, Ulrich Bunke gave an explicit description of their smooth
            = succeed "-theory" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathcal{E}, \rho)
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            is a ‘geometric family’, namely a smooth proper submersion
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \rightarrow B
            with a fiberwise metric, a Clifford bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            and some connection of some sort. The object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
            is an element of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi Λ
                      %mo *
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mo *
                    %mi B
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msup
                      %mi K
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega(B, K) = C^\infty(B, \Lambda^*T^* B \otimes K^*)
            = succeed "-orientatiosn" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            = succeed "-invariant" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
          %h2#christoph_schweigert_and_ingo_runkel_cft_and_algebra_in_braided_tensor_categories_i_and_ii
            Christoph Schweigert and Ingo Runkel:
            %em CFT and algebra in braided tensor categories I and II
          %ul
            %li
              See also
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Oberwolfach+Workshop%2C+June+2009+--+Monday%2C+June+8"} notes from the conference
          %p
            This talk explains how correlators for a rational two-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+field+theory"} conformal field theory
            can be constructed in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} functorial TFT
            formalism. Based on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+tensor+category"} modular tensor category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and the correlator, as an element of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi tft
                    %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} tft_C (\hat{X})
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat{X}
            a double cover of the surface
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_X
            with
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∂
                    %msub
                      %mi M
                      %mi X
                    %mo =
                    %mover
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial M_X = \hat{X}
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+class+group"} mapping class group
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sewing+constraint"} sewing constraint
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morita+equivalence"} Morita equivalent
            special symemtric Frobenius algebras lead to an equivalent description of the correlators. A Morita invariant formulation is provided by the notion of a module category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            over the module tensor category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} world sheet
            is now decorated by categories, functors between module categories and natural transformations.
          %h2#kevin_costello_factorization_algebras_in_perturbative_qft
            Kevin Costello:
            %em Factorization algebras in perturbative QFT
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Oberwolfach+Workshop%2C+June+2009+--+Monday%2C+June+8"}> Talk 1
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Oberwolfach+Workshop%2C+June+2009+--+Tuesday%2C+June+9"} Talk 2
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Oberwolfach+Workshop%2C+June+2009+--+Tuesday%2C+June+9"} Talk 3
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+algebra"} factorization algebra
            on a manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            is an object which associates to every ball
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \subseteq M
            a vector space (or cochain complex)
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(B)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mi n
                  %mo ⊂
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_1 \coprod \cdots \coprod B_n \subset B_{n+1}
            of disjoint balls in a larger ball, a map
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(B_1) \otimes \cdots \otimes F(B_n) \rightarrow F(B_{n+1})
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty
            analogues of chiral algebras, as introduced by Beilinson and Drinfeld; they are closely related to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+over+an+operad"} algebras
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/little+n-disk+operad"} little n-disk operad
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_n
          %p
            In this series of lectures, Kevin Costello set up a framework to construct
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+algebra"} factorization algebras
            from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+quantization"} deformation quantization
            picture of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable"} observable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            are encoded in an associative algebra, Costello argues that the observables of a QFT on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            are encoded in a factorization algebra on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
          %p This series of lectures finished with the statement of a theorem allowing one to quantize the commutative factorization algebra associated to a classical field theory in a range of situations, including situations of physical interest. This is joint work with Owen Gwilliam.
          %h2#_operads__and_
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gabriel+C.+Drummond-Cole"}> Gabriel C. Drummond-Cole
            \:
            %em
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              \-Operads,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi BU
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} BU_\infty
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Hypercomm
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hypercomm_\infty
          %ul
            %li
              See also
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Oberwolfach+Workshop%2C+June+2009+--+Wednesday%2C+June+10"} notes from the conference
          %p
            The speaker applies the machinery of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operad"} operad
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∂
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} overbar
                  %mo ∂
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial-\overbar{\partial}
            lemma to hypercommutative algebras (Frobenius manifolds). This is joint work with Bruno Valette. The following theorem was proved:
          .un_thm
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              be a differential BV-algebra over a field of characteristic zero. Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              be its homology. Then: 1. If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              satisfies the noncommutative Hodge to de Rham degeneration condition, then there exists a hypercommutative
              = succeed "-structure" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              satisfies Park’s semiclassical condition then this structure is unique up to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Hypercomm
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hypercomm_\infty
              quasi-isomorphism.
          %h2#alex_kahle_superconnections_and_index_theory
            Alex Kahle:
            %em Superconnections and index theory
          %ul
            %li
              See also
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Oberwolfach+Workshop%2C+June+2009+--+Wednesday%2C+June+10"} notes from the conference
          %p
            In this talk, Alex Kahle described results from his thesis work (his supervisor was Dan Freed) about
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0810.0820"} superconnections and index theory
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superconnection"} superconnection
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} ordinary connection
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+operator"} Dirac operator
            on a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinor+bundle"} spinor bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo →
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \rightarrow 0
            to a certain differential form) needs to be modified though, because forms of different degrees have different scaling behaviour (recall that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superconnection"} superconnection
            involves forms of different degrees). Alex worked out how to scale everything correctly so that one indeed gets a local index theorem for superconnections, leading to many potential applications.
          %h2#_topological_defects_branes_and_the_classifications_of_tfts_in_low_dimensions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chris+Schommer-Pries"}> Chris Schommer-Pries
            \:
            %em
              Topological defects,
              = succeed "-branes," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "http://sites.google.com/site/chrisschommerpriesmath/Home/Slides-MFO-6-11-09.pdf?attredirects=0"} Slides on webpage
          %p
            Chris explained his classification result mentioned at
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2Cn%29-category+of+cobordisms"} (infinity,n)-category of cobordisms
            from his thesis of extended (he suggested calling them
            = succeed ")" do
              %em local
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                    %mi Cob
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2Cob
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher-categorical viewpoint
            unites the following two ideas: the ‘open-closed’ theories and the ‘field theories with defects’ from Ingo Runkel and Christoph Schweigert’s talk. He showed how both these concepts are particular examples of a single notion, namely that of a collection of natural transformations between a restriction of
            %span.newWikiWord
              FTQFT
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/FTQFT"} ?
            = succeed "-functors" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            This is precisely the kind of viewpoint that Urs Schreiber and Jens Fjelstad
            %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2007/08/dbranes_from_tin_cans_part_x.html"} suggested
            at the
            %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/"} n-category cafe
            under the slogal
            %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2006/10/dbranes_from_tin_cans_arrow_th.html"} D-branes from tin-cans
            (a D-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brane"} brane
            is a boundary condition ordefect, a “tin-can diagram” is the naturality diagram for a higher natural transformation).
          %p By the way, there’s much more to come from Chris, he is working on the 3d theory and much besides!
          %h2#dan_freed_geometry_and_topology_of_orientifolds
            Dan Freed:
            %em Geometry and topology of orientifolds
          %ul
            %li
              See also
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Oberwolfach+Workshop%2C+June+2009+--+Monday%2C+June+8"} notes from the conference
          %p
            Freed described recent work with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacques+Distler"} Jacques Distler
            and Greg Moore on a certain background structure in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} superstring theory
            called an
            %span.newWikiWord
              orientifold], which is a [[bundle gerbe
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/orientifold%5D%2C+which+is+a+%5B%5Bbundle+gerbe"} ?
            on a
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}_2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifold"} orbifold
            with a peculiar “twisted”
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariance"} equivariance
            condition. There is apparantly a beautiful string theory formula to calculate the ‘
            %span.newWikiWord>
              RR charge
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/RR+charge"} ?
            ’, namely something like
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi RR
                  %mi charge
                  %mo =
                  %mo ±
                  %msup
                    %mn 2
                    %mi something
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mo *
                  %msqrt
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                          %mi L
                          %mo ′
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi F
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mrow
                          %mi L
                          %mo ′
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi ν
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  RR charge = \pm 2^{something} i_* \sqrt\left( \frac{L'(F)}{L'(\nu)} \right)
          %p
            involving a certain normal bundle and the ‘Bott element in K-theory’, which looks similar to the Hirzebruch formula for the L-genus. This uses some kind of twisted
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
            version of KR theory. The whole thing can be viewed as a new sort of anomaly, having to do with an exotic orientation of some kind.
          %h2#scott_wilson_some_algebra_relat4ed_to_mapping_spaces_and_applications
            Scott Wilson:
            %em Some algebra relat4ed to mapping spaces and applications
          %ul
            %li
              See also
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Oberwolfach+Workshop%2C+June+2009+--+Wednesday%2C+June+10"} notes from the conference
          %p
            Wilson defined a
            %em partial algebra
            as a lax
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+functor"} monoidal functor
            from the category of finite sets to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} chain complexes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi j
                  %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A(j \coprod k) \rightarrow A(j) \otimes A(k)
            is a quasi-isomorphism. He showed how these pop up in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            all the time.
          %p
            (Remark: this formula is the basis for
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"} Jacob Lurie
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+algebra+in+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} commutative algebra in an (infinity,1)-category
            in general and of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} symmetric monoidal (infinity,1)-category
            in particular).
          %p
            For instance, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            is any space and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            a partial algebra, the resulting total complex can be seen as a generalization of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hochschild+cohomology"} Hochschild cohomology
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bruce+Bartlett"} Bruce
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            is the interval, and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is the partial algebra of forms on a Riemannian manifold
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
          %h2#_background_fields_in_twisted_differential_nonabelian_cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"}> Urs Schreiber
            \:
            %em Background fields in twisted differential nonabelian cohomology
          %ul
            %li
              See also
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Oberwolfach+Workshop%2C+June+2009+--+Thursday%2C+June+11"} notes from the conference
            %li
              detailed notes are at
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Background+fields+in+twisted+differential+nonabelian+cohomology"} Background fields in twisted differential nonabelian cohomology
          %p
            What can I (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bruce+Bartlett"}> Bruce
            ) say about Urs’s talk? Many mere mortals might think it impressive to pass from ordinary cohomology to twisted cohomology, or to differential cohomology, or to nonabelian cohomology. Urs does this all in one step! Yes, we’re talking about
            = succeed "." do
              %em twisted differential nonabelian cohomology
            = succeed "-machinery" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is related to
            %em twisted
            abelian cohomology on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %h2#andr_henriques_invertible_conformal_nets
            André Henriques:
            %em Invertible conformal nets
          %ul
            %li
              See also
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Oberwolfach+Workshop%2C+June+2009+--+Thursday%2C+June+11"} notes from the conference
          %p
            In this talk, Henriques described joint work with Chris Douglas and Arthur Bartels on the program of establishing an equivalence between full conformal field theories and ‘conformal nets’. Preliminary write-ups of this work is available on his
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.uu.nl/people/henrique/"} webpage
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+algebra"} factorization algebra
            (in the sense of Costello’s talk) on 1-dimensional manifolds taking values in the bicategory of Von Neumann algebras with
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bimodule"} bimodule
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            %em
              index
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(A)
              of a conformal net
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is defined to be the statistical dimension of the vacuum sector
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi H
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_0
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              assigns to arcs on the unit circle. He stated the following result.
          .un_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+net"} conformal net
              is invertible if and only if
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi μ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(A)=1
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi μ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(A) \lt \infty
          %h2#_string_connections_and_chernsimons_2gerbes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Konrad+Waldorf"}> Konrad Waldorf
            \:
            %em String connections and Chern-Simons 2-gerbes
          %ul
            %li
              See also
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Oberwolfach+Workshop%2C+June+2009+--+Thursday%2C+June+11"} notes from the conference
          %p
            Konrad described the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+2-gerbe"} Chern-Simons 2-gerbe
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi CS
                    %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CS_P
            which is a geometric object living on any manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            which is equipped with a Spin-bundle
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
            of the basic bundle gerbe over the Spin group. He demonstrates that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+structure"} string structure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is a
            %em trivialization
            of this 2-gerbe, in the sense of a morphism from the trivial 2-gerbe living over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi CS
                      %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CS_P
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 2
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{1}{2} p_1(P) = 0
            %em string connection
            on a string structure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            (a connection on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is needed here) on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            as a connection on this trivialization
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            which is compatible with various things.
          %p
            Basically, the data of a string structure together with a string connection on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is a class in the
            %em
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi CS
                      %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [CS_P]
              \-twisted
            differential cohomology of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            in Urs’s sense, for vanishing twist (the twist is the nontriviality of the String structure). Konrad described various results about string connections, such as the fact that they form a contractible space, confirming a conjecture by Stolz and Teichner. See his recent
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0906.0117"} arXiv article
          %h2#kevin_walker_blob_homology
            Kevin Walker:
            = succeed "." do
              %em Blob homology
          %ul
            %li
              Slides available on
              %a{:href => "http://canyon23.net/math"} webpage
          %p
            Kevin Walker described a new way to think about
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} extended TQFTs
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            and an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-category"} n-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            he defined a ‘
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/blob+complex"}> blob complex
            ’
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_*(M, C)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z(M)
            to manifolds of dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \leq n
            are produced out of this chain complex, with the Atiyah-Segal axioms following as corollaries of the setup. He listed a number of examples of the TQFT’s he has in mind in the beginning, such as the finite group model, one based on a
            %em
              \-algebra, one based on a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pivotal+category"} pivotal category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braided+monoidal+category"} braided
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ribbon+category"} ribbon category
          %h2#ralph_cohen_string_topology_field_theories_and_fukaya_categories
            Ralph Cohen:
            %em String topology, field theories and Fukaya categories
          %ul
            %li No notes!?
          %p
            This talk was about relating the subject of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+topology"} string topology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} String theory
            was described as a ‘homological
            = succeed "\u2019." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+field+theory"} conformal field theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gromov-Witten+theory"} Gromov-Witten symplectic field theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi T
                    %mo *
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^*M
            is equivalent to string topology of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
          %h2#corbett_redden_string_structures_3forms_and_tmf_classes
            Corbett Redden:
            %em String structures, 3-forms, and tmf classes
          %ul
            %li
              See also
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Oberwolfach+Workshop%2C+June+2009+--+Thursday%2C+June+11"} notes from the conference
          %p
            Corbett began my defining a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+structure"} string structure
            on a manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            as a certain lift of a classifying map; string structures up to homotopy are canonically isomorphic with things called ‘string classes’ on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            \- namely, elements in the third integral cohomology of the total space of the bundle which behave on the fibers as the generator of the third cohomology of the Spin group. The point about string structures is that they transgress to give a spin structure on the loop space of the manifold, which is what one wants to study things like the Witten genus. He showed a beautiful way to obtain string structures from a metric on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi M
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M^n
            admits a spin and a string structure and a Riemannian metric
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            such that the Ricci curvature of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            is positive and that
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H=0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma(M, S)=0
            inside
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi tmf
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi pt
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} tmf^{-n}(pt)
          %h2#mike_hopkins_the_kervaire_invariant
            Mike Hopkins:
            %em The Kervaire invariant
          %ul
            %li
              See also
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Oberwolfach+Workshop%2C+June+2009+--+Thursday%2C+June+11"} notes from the conference
          %p
            Hopkins gave a nice story of the history and origins of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kervaire+invariant"} Kervaire invariant
            in the classification of
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            = succeed "!" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n=2
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %a{:href => "webpage"} http://www.maths.ed.ac.uk/~aar/exotic.htm
            on exotic spheres and Douglas Ravenel’s
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.rochester.edu/u/faculty/doug/"} webpage
